    {
      "title": "Do not substitute a generic full refund for the statute",
      "text": "Section 16-105 describes the ten-day post-delivery notice with written cancellation and a pro rata refund for the unexpired term, or an approved notice no less favorable. It has a specified employment-plan exception. A policy offering an unconditional full refund is more favorable and its actual notice matters. The covered replacement rule in COMAR 31.09.05.06 is separate.",
      "example": "The buyer-guide delivery exception for an unconditional refund provision of at least ten days does not rewrite §16-105 into a universal full-refund mandate.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A payment mode is not paid-up status",
      "text": "Annual, semiannual, quarterly and monthly describe payment frequency. Compare the total required cost and schedule. A limited-pay or single-premium design defines when its required premium obligation is fulfilled; a monthly installment under a continuing-pay policy does not do that.",
      "example": "Twelve payments of $55 cost $660; four payments of $160 cost $640. These are arithmetic assumptions, not industry rates.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Grace periods depend on the contract category",
      "text": "Section 16-202 provides thirty days, or an insurer-selected month of at least thirty days, for the standard individual-life provision. Industrial life with premiums more frequent than monthly has four weeks. Group life has a separate 31-day standard provision taught in Part 16. First-premium and conditional-receipt questions do not automatically receive renewal-premium grace.",
      "example": "Do not answer an individual-life thirty-day question with the group 31-day rule simply because both involve life insurance.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Universal and variable life can require another notice",
      "text": "If a universal or variable policy permits a face-amount reduction, §16-219 requires a notice identifying that option, the amount needed to avoid lapse and the customer-service number. It goes to the last known address at the start of grace and at least thirty days before termination. The provision does not guarantee every policy remains funded forever without payment.",
      "example": "The owner should review available coverage and funding options before the stated termination.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Incontestability is not unlimited coverage",
      "text": "Section 16-203 measures two years in force during the insured’s lifetime, excepts nonpayment and specifies separate treatment of disability and additional accidental-death provisions. It bars the specified validity contest, while allowing defenses based on actual coverage exclusions or restrictions. Reinstatement and particular policy changes have additional rules taught later.",
      "example": "A claim filed thirty months after issue when death occurred at eighteen months does not satisfy the two-year lifetime condition.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A contestable claim is reviewed under legal standards",
      "text": "Maryland §12-207 does not make every inaccurate application answer an automatic forfeiture. Its fraud, materiality and good-faith underwriting alternatives must be applied to the facts. Conversely, an inaccurate statement is not always harmless merely because the applicant says there was no intent to deceive.",
      "example": "Determine what the answer was, why it mattered and whether the policy remains contestable; avoid deciding from one label alone.",
      "topics": [
        "5.1.08"
      ]
    }
